1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Hey everyone! Welcome to what might be the most important video

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:07,000
in this entire series.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,000
We are covering backups, restores, and troubleshooting.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,000
I know - it is not the flashiest topic.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,000
But imagine losing all your customer data,
your deals, your inventory -

6
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,000
because you did not have a backup.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,000
That is a nightmare nobody wants.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,000
In this video, I am going to show you how to back up
your VentureX installation,

9
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,000
how to restore it if something goes wrong,

10
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,000
and how to fix the most common issues people run into.

11
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:36,000
Stick around - this could save your business.

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Let us start with the most critical backup -
your database.

13
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:44,000
This is where all your contacts, deals,
invoices, and inventory live.

14
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,000
If you have SSH access - that is a terminal connection
to your server - this is the fastest way.

15
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,000
Log into your server, then run:

16
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,000
mysqldump -u vxuser -p venturex_prod
and redirect output to a backup file with the date in the name.

17
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,000
Enter your database password when prompted.

18
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:01,000
It will create a file with today's date and time -
like backup_20260820_143022.sql.

19
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,000
That file contains your entire database.

20
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,000
Download it to your computer for safekeeping
using SCP or your FTP client.

21
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:12,000
Now, if you do not have SSH access -
you are on shared hosting with just cPanel -

22
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,000
go to phpMyAdmin in cPanel.

23
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,000
Select your database on the left.

24
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,000
Click the Export tab at the top.

25
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:24,000
Choose Quick export, SQL format, and click Go.

26
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:27,000
It downloads the .sql file to your computer.
Same result, different method.

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,000
I recommend doing this at least once a week,
or better yet, set up an automated script.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Let us automate this so you never forget.

29
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,000
On a VPS, create a backup script.

30
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,000
Run nano ~/backup.sh and paste in a script
that dumps the database with mysqldump,

31
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,000
names the file with today's date,

32
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,000
and deletes backups older than 30 days
so your server does not fill up.

33
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,000
Save it, then make it executable:
chmod +x ~/backup.sh.

34
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,000
Add a cron job: crontab -e, then add a line
to run the script every day at 2 AM.

35
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,000
On cPanel, you can do something similar -
create the script in File Manager,

36
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:03,000
then add a cron job through the Cron Jobs section.

37
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,000
Backups are now automatic. Sleep well at night.

38
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Your database is not the only thing that needs backing up.

39
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,000
You have also got uploaded files, config,
and customizations.

40
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,000
Here is what to back up.

41
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:19,000
First, your .env file - this has all your configuration.

42
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:23,000
Second, the storage/app directory - that is where
uploaded documents, profile photos, and exports live.

43
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,000
Third, any custom themes or modifications
you have made.

44
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,000
On a VPS, use tar to create a complete backup:

45
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:35,000
tar -czf with the backup path, then the directories
for .env, storage/app, and public/uploads.

46
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:39,000
That creates a compressed archive
of just the important files.

47
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,000
On shared hosting, open File Manager,
navigate to your project folder,

48
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,000
select .env, storage, and any custom folders,
right-click, and Compress.

49
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:48,000
Download the zip file.

50
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:52,000
I recommend doing file backups monthly,
or whenever you make significant changes.

51
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:56,000
Store your backups somewhere other than the server -
an external hard drive, Google Drive, Dropbox, or S3.

52
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,000
If your server dies, your backups should survive.

53
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Alright, let us say the worst happened.
Your site is down

54
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,000
and you need to restore from backup.

55
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,000
Do not panic - here is how.

56
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,000
First, the database.

57
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,000
If you have SSH:
mysql -u vxuser -p venturex_prod < backup.sql.

58
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,000
Enter the password, and it imports everything.

59
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:23,000
If you are on shared hosting, go to phpMyAdmin,
select your database, click the Import tab,

60
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,000
choose your .sql file, and click Go.

61
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,000
Wait for it to finish -
you will see a success message.

62
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,000
Next, restore your files.

63
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,000
If you backed up with tar:
tar -xzf the backup file to the root directory.

64
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:40,000
If you compressed via File Manager,
upload the zip, extract it,

65
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,000
and make sure the files land in the right directories.

66
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,000
After restoring, run a few cleanup commands
if you have terminal access:

67
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,000
php artisan config:cache,
php artisan route:cache,

68
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:53,000
php artisan view:cache.

69
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:56,000
Clear any old cache: php artisan cache:clear.

70
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,000
Check your site.

71
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,000
It should be back to exactly where it was
when you made the backup.

72
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:06,000
This is why backups matter - a bad day becomes
a minor inconvenience instead of a catastrophe.

73
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Let us go through the most common issues
people hit during installation

74
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:13,000
and how to fix each one.

75
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,000
Issue one - 500 Internal Server Error.
This is the most common one.

76
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,000
Almost always, it is a file permissions problem.

77
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:24,000
Fix: set the bootstrap/cache and storage directories
to 755.

78
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:28,000
Run: chmod -R 755 bootstrap/cache storage.

79
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:31,000
Also check your .env file exists -
if it is missing, copy .env.example to .env.

80
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Issue two - blank white page.

81
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,000
This is usually PHP errors being hidden.

82
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:42,000
Fix: in your .env file, set APP_DEBUG=true temporarily.

83
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,000
Reload the page - now you will see
the actual error message.

84
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:49,000
Fix the error, then set APP_DEBUG back to false
for production.

85
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:53,000
Issue three - Class not found errors.

86
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:56,000
This means composer dependencies are not installed.

87
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:59,000
Fix: run composer install in your project root.

88
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,000
If you get memory errors, run:
composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader --no-scripts.

89
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Issue four - database connection refused.

90
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:11,000
Check your .env - DB_HOST is usually localhost or 127.0.0.1,
not localhost:3307.

91
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,000
Make sure the database name, username, and password
are exactly right - one wrong character and it fails silently.

92
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Cannot log in? Here are the fixes.

93
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:23,000
Problem - you forgot your password.

94
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:27,000
If you have terminal access, run php artisan tinker,
then update the user password using Hash::make.

95
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:30,000
Exit tinker with Ctrl+D.

96
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:34,000
If you do not have terminal, many versions have
a password reset link on the login page.

97
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:38,000
Problem - login page just reloads without error.

98
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:42,000
This is usually a session or cookie issue.

99
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:46,000
Clear your browser cookies for the site,
try a different browser,

100
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:50,000
or check that your APP_URL in .env
matches exactly what you are visiting

101
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:53,000
- http vs https matters,
and trailing slashes matter.

102
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,000
Problem - your account has been disabled.

103
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:01,000
Check with your admin - accounts can be deactivated
from the Settings panel.

104
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:05,000
If you are the only admin, use the database reset
to set is_active back to 1.

105
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:09,000
Problem - stuck in a redirect loop after login.

106
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:13,000
Fix: make sure SESSION_DRIVER in .env
is set to database or file,

107
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:16,000
not redis if Redis is not installed.

108
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
If the AI features are not working,
it is usually one of three things.

109
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:24,000
First - API key not configured.

110
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:27,000
Go to Settings, then AI Settings.

111
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:31,000
Make sure your OpenAI API key is entered correctly.
It should start with sk-.

112
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:35,000
If you just see asterisks, delete and re-enter it.
Test it with the Test Connection button.

113
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:39,000
Second - API key has no credits.

114
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:42,000
Log into your OpenAI account and check your billing.

115
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:46,000
If you have hit your usage limit,
add credits or upgrade your plan.

116
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:50,000
Third - queue worker is not running.

117
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:54,000
The AI features use background processing.
On VPS, check: sudo supervisorctl status.

118
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:58,000
If the worker is stopped, start it
with sudo supervisorctl start.

119
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:02,000
On shared hosting, make sure your cron job
for php artisan schedule:run is active.

120
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:06,000
If none of that works, check the
storage/logs/laravel.log file

121
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:09,000
- it will show the exact error
the AI module is throwing.

122
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:13,000
Most AI errors are API-related
and resolve once the key and billing are sorted.

123
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Let us make VentureX faster.

124
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:21,000
Tip one - enable caching.
If you are on VPS, make sure OPcache is on.

125
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:25,000
Run: php -m | grep opcache.
Set opcache.revalidate_freq to 2 in your PHP config.

126
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:28,000
Tip two - optimize your database.

127
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:32,000
Remove old logs periodically:
php artisan log:clear.

128
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:36,000
Tip three - use queue workers for heavy tasks.
Instead of sending emails during the page load,

129
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:39,000
queue them. This makes the interface snappy for users.

130
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:43,000
Tip four - optimize your assets.
Run: php artisan view:cache and php artisan config:cache.

131
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:47,000
This pre-compiles views and caches configuration
so Laravel does not rebuild them on every request.

132
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:51,000
Tip five - monitor your server.
Install htop on VPS: sudo apt install htop.

133
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:54,000
Run htop to see CPU and memory usage in real-time.

134
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:58,000
If MySQL is eating too much RAM,
reduce innodb_buffer_pool_size.

135
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:02,000
If PHP-FPM is maxing out,
increase pm.max_children in the PHP-FPM pool config.

136
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:06,000
Tip six - use a CDN for static assets.

137
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,000
Cloudflare free tier works great -
just point your DNS to Cloudflare and enable their proxy.

138
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:14,000
These changes together can cut your page load time in half.

139
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
If you have tried everything
and you are still stuck,

140
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:21,000
here is where to get help.

141
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:24,000
First, check the documentation -
most answers are there.

142
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:28,000
Second, search the GitHub issues page -
someone else probably hit the same problem.

143
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:32,000
Third, check the community forum or Discord server -
other users and the development team are active there.

144
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:36,000
When you post for help, include these details:
your PHP version, your VentureX version,

145
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:39,000
the exact error message you are seeing,
and what you have already tried.

146
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,000
Screenshots help too.

147
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,000
The more information you give,
the faster you will get a solution.

148
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,000
And remember - backup before you try major fixes.
That way if something breaks further,

149
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:53,000
you can restore and try a different approach.

150
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,000
And that brings us to the end of this video -
and the end of this 15-part VentureX series!

151
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:02,000
Let us recap what we covered.

152
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:06,000
We started with getting to know VentureX,
checked the system requirements,

153
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,000
set up the database, configured the environment,
installed the application,

154
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:12,000
and took our first steps with the CRM.

155
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:16,000
Then we dove into inventory management,
explored the AI features,

156
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:18,000
and configured settings.

157
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:22,000
We deployed to cPanel and VPS,
connected WordPress, Wix, and Blogger,

158
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:26,000
and now we know how to back up, restore, and troubleshoot.

159
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:30,000
You now have everything you need to run
a professional ERP and CRM system for your business.

160
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:34,000
If this series helped you,
share it with someone who could use it.

161
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,000
And if you have questions or feature requests,
drop them in the comments or reach out on GitHub.

162
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:42,000
Thanks for watching, and best of luck with your business!
